WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.160
A crisis of confidence.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.340
Automobile production dropped 37 and a half percent.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.580
Wall Street reacted very badly.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.940
If you were looking at a very    specific type of line.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.780
You see, normally, this line points slightly
upward — like here, in September 1977.

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:28.039
But about a year later, it started pointing
the other way, just slightly!

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:29.689
And then not long after that...

00:00:29.689 --> 00:00:30.689
Boom.

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:32.239
The 1980 recession.

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:34.250
It happened again a few months later.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.620
The line curved downward.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.540
Then, boom, another recession.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.579
And it happened again in 1988.

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:45.100
Again in 2000.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.449
And again in the mid-2000s.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.480
This line is called the “yield curve.”

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.720
And that’s why some experts freaked out
when this happened ...

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.560
"A recession warning."

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:00.920
"Inverted yield curve."

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:01.840
"An inverted yield curve."

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:02.840
"Inverted yield curve."

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.660
So.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.860
What the hell is this line?

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.240
It all starts with a US treasury bond.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.300
A bond is basically an agreement saying: If
you lend the federal government, say, a hundred

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.970
dollars...
… they'll pay you interest while they hold

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:22.260
onto your money...
… until the date they agreed to pay you

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.260
back.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.970
And the longer you let the government keep
your money, the higher the interest rate.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.360
So you get more money.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.220
Next you need to understand that most people
don't buy bonds from the government.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:39.150
They buy and sell them from each other, in
the secondary market.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:45.800
And the prices change, based on how much demand
there is for a bond.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.460
This basically means that the amount of profit
you can make on each bond changes every day.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.140
Trace these bars on any given day, and you
get a curved line, showing the yields of different

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.260
bonds — or what people call "the yield curve."

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.180
And normally, it points upward.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.750
Now here’s where it gets even more complicated.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.110
Let's say you're an investor...
and you have a hunch that an economic downturn

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.160
is coming, in the near future.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.700
If your hunch is correct, that means that
if you buy a two-year bond, you might get

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.280
your money back in a bad economy,
and there might not be anything good to re-invest in.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.380
That makes a two-year bond a lot less attractive
to you.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:32.670
And if lots of other people think this way,
then the demand for two-year bonds plummets.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.320
So they start selling for cheaper.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:43.220
But because the two-year bond now costs less,
it yields a better return, relative to that low cost.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.160
And at the same time, investors who think
a downturn is coming might think, I'd rather

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.329
invest in a 10-year bond that pays out way
later — when I think the economic downturn

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:52.639
will be over.

00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:54.020
So that bond gets more popular.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.300
But it also gets more expensive.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.360
So investors start yielding less money.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.920
And if enough investors are acting on this
expectation, the yield on a long-term bond,

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.799
which is almost always higher than on a short-term
bond, can actually dip lower.

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:14.810
And if you draw that yield curve...
… you can see it goes in the other direction.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.160
It inverts.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.340
In other words, when this chart looks like
this, it means investors think an economic

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.440
downturn is probably coming in the near future.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.690
And that's what's happening now.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.640
So, is a recession coming?

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.280
Not necessarily.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:34.900
But when re-design the chart so we can see
all the years on a single screen…

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:42.260
… it's pretty safe to say: When the yield
curve inverts… it's not a good sign.

